        </p>

        <p>
            In addition to instant conversion, this page explains the binary number system, decimal notation, conversion formulas, examples, and frequently asked questions. By understanding the logic behind binary to decimal conversion, you can strengthen your programming, mathematics, and digital technology skills.
        </p>

        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-4">What Is a Binary Number?</h2>

        <p>
            A binary number is a number expressed using only two digits: <strong>0</strong> and <strong>1</strong>. Unlike the decimal number system, which uses ten digits (0–9), the binary system uses a base of 2. Each digit in a binary number is called a binary digit or bit.
        </p>

        <p>
            Binary numbers are the foundation of modern computing because electronic devices naturally operate using two states: on and off. These states can be represented by 1 and 0, making binary an ideal language for computers. Every image, video, application, document, and website stored on your computer is ultimately represented using binary data.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>0 = 0</li>
            <li>1 = 1</li>
            <li>10 = 2</li>
            <li>11 = 3</li>
            <li>100 = 4</li>
            <li>101 = 5</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            Each position in a binary number represents a power of two. Reading from right to left, the positions correspond to 2⁰, 2¹, 2², 2³, 2⁴, and so on. This positional system allows binary numbers to represent any numerical value.
        </p>

        <p>
            Binary numbers are used extensively in programming, digital electronics, networking, cybersecurity, telecommunications, embedded systems, and computer architecture. Understanding binary notation is essential for anyone studying computer science or working in technology-related fields.
        </p>

        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-4">What Is a Decimal Number?</h2>

        <p>
            The decimal number system is the standard numbering system used in everyday life. It uses ten digits ranging from 0 to 9 and is based on powers of ten. Because humans have historically counted using ten fingers, the decimal system became the dominant numerical system worldwide.
        </p>

        <p>
            Examples of decimal numbers include:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>5</li>
            <li>10</li>
            <li>25</li>
            <li>100</li>
            <li>500</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            Each position in a decimal number represents a power of ten. For example, the number 356 contains:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>3 × 100</li>
            <li>5 × 10</li>
            <li>6 × 1</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            Because humans primarily work with decimal values while computers operate using binary, conversion between binary and decimal is frequently required in programming and digital systems.
        </p>

        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-4">How to Convert Binary to Decimal</h2>

        <p>
            Converting binary to decimal involves multiplying each binary digit by the corresponding power of two and then adding the results together.
        </p>

        <p>
            Let's convert the binary number <strong>1010</strong>.
        </p>

        <table class="table table-bordered">
            <tr>
                <th>Binary Digit</th>
                <th>Power of 2</th>
                <th>Calculation</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>2³</td>
                <td>8</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>0</td>
                <td>2²</td>
                <td>0</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>2¹</td>
                <td>2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>0</td>
                <td>2⁰</td>
                <td>0</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            Adding the values:
        </p>

        <p>
            8 + 0 + 2 + 0 = 10
        </p>

        <p>
            Therefore:
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong>1010₂ = 10₁₀</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
            This method works for all binary numbers regardless of length. The binary to decimal calculator on this page performs this calculation instantly and accurately.
        </p>

        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-4">Binary to Decimal Formula</h2>

        <p>
            The mathematical formula for converting binary to decimal is:
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-light border">
            Decimal = (b₀ × 2⁰) + (b₁ × 2¹) + (b₂ × 2²) + (b₃ × 2³) + ...
        </div>

        <p>
            Where:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>b₀, b₁, b₂ represent binary digits</li>
            <li>Each digit is multiplied by a power of 2</li>
            <li>The results are summed together</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            This formula forms the basis of all binary to decimal conversion calculators and is widely used in computer science education.
        </p>

